<<~ ahu #/head>>

! Managed identity ~ the measured abuse surface

A tasked spirit went after the abuse surface that opens when ''one identity manages another''. Two headline answers, both stated plainly:

> ''Has anyone built an exit the manager cannot block? NO.'' Across every shipped supervised-identity system and every SSI guardianship design reached, the subject's exit gates on the manager's cooperation, an age threshold plus a manager-controlled toggle, or an external legal process.
>
> ''What breaks when management goes structural? The AUTHORIZATION GRAPH becomes the abuse surface, and malware falls to a rounding error.''

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/the-graph-is-the-surface>>

!! ★ The surface measures as the authorization graph, not the software ★

A clinic serving 44 clients across 105 devices found:

|!detected |!count |
|potential ''spyware'' |''3'' |
|potential password compromise |14 |
|''presence of unknown "trusted" devices'' |''12'' |
|shared family / phone plan |4 |
|clients with ≥1 vulnerability |''23 of 44 (52%)'' |

''Relation-layer findings outnumbered spyware roughly 10:1'' — and the scanner did not find them. //"In some cases, the TAQ and technograph were the primary (or only) way to uncover a potential problem"// — a ''hand-drawn map of people, devices and accounts''.

The clinic's own explanation: //"many abusers are seemingly able to surveil clients via compromised accounts, and so may not need to install spyware."//

!!! And the tooling blocklist says the same thing at scale

''5,777 flagged apps: dual-use 5,150 (89.1%) · stalkerware 613 · spyware 13.'' By store: ''99.2% ON-store''. And ''581 (10.1%) carry `family|parent|kid|child|teen` in the title'', 569 of them flagged dual-use.

> ''The clinic's operational model of the abuse surface reads 89% legitimate-purpose software.''

⚠ ''Making management structural does not ADD an app to the threat model. It IS the threat model.''

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/nobody-verifies-the-manager>>

!! ★ Nobody verifies who the manager is ~ and this lands on Ruling 2b ★

The spirit's own statement of what changes the shape of the design:

> ''Nobody verifies who the manager is.'' None of 40 parental-control apps implemented multi-guardian consent; vendors assert //"only the device owner's consent is required"// — while ''device ownership extends beyond legal guardianship''; and a regulator's own remedy accepts ''an unverified self-attestation checkbox''.
>
> ⚠ ''A design where the manager is a persona with no human owner-of-record removes the last thing every existing system nominally relies on — an attributable person to attest.''

''Read that against `persona-policy#the-managed-persona` directly.'' A Persona managing a PersonaGroup, with no human bound anywhere, is precisely the shape this names. The refusal to bind a human buys the privacy property AND spends the only accountability anchor the field has, ''and the field's anchor was already a checkbox nobody checked''.

⚠ So the question the model inherits reads: ''what stands in for attestation when no person stands behind the manager?'' Nothing here answers it, and the sweep found no system that answers it either.

!!! The one mitigation adversaries visibly complained about

Not a UI notice. ''An identity-layer role check'' — one product verified the age of the account a device was registered to, and //"abusers complain in reviews… about the difficulty in using it"// for surveillance. ''The thing that worked lived in the identity layer.''

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/the-three-properties>>

!! ★ The design axis ~ three separable properties, and nothing holds all three ★

> ''The design axis is not visible-versus-invisible.'' It is three separable properties, and ''no shipped system or paper does all three'':
>
> # the management relation ''ENUMERABLE by the managed, and by a third party they choose''
> # the alert ''NOT SWITCHABLE by the manager''
> # the severance ''NOT ANNOUNCED to the manager''

Property ② already exists as a written commitment in two places that never applied it to themselves — an anti-tracking standard's //"any mode that makes it possible to turn off Unwanted Tracking Alerts is antithetical… should not be circumventable under any circumstance"//, and a store policy compelling ''a persistent notification at all times'' plus a machine-checkable manifest flag on every third-party monitoring app.

⚠ ''And the platforms wrote that rule for everyone but themselves.'' The same store forbids monitoring even a CONSENTING spouse — while its own family product ships ''no documented ambient indicator''. The other vendor writes no such rule and ships the cleanest silent supervision: a restricted app is //"just temporarily hidden from the Home Screen"// — no notice, no attribution. ''The relation stays visible in settings; every individual exercise of it stays invisible.''

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/the-two-directions>>

!! ⚠ Visibility and severance pull opposite ways, and both are measured

* ''Non-enumerability reads as a documented harm channel with NO counterexample.'' Every measured case where a relation could not be enumerated produced unrecoverable adversarial persistence. Nothing shows non-enumerability protecting anyone.
* ''AND VISIBILITY IS NOT FREE.'' Severance that notifies the manager measures as an ''escalation trigger'', by four independent sources — including the anti-tracking standard's own threat model: //"taking overt security measures while frequently in physical proximity to the Attacker may lead to the Attacker escalating their tactics of interpersonal control."//

> ''Visibility of the management relation and visibility of its SEVERANCE carry opposite signs. Any design that couples them inherits the worse half of both.''

!!! The nearest quantitative test points sideways

A meta-analysis of ''94 independent samples, N = 23,461'' on monitoring in another setting: //"no evidence that [monitoring] improves worker performance"//; //"the presence of [monitoring] increases worker stress and strain, **regardless of the characteristics of monitoring**"//. Transparency raised how people FELT about the monitor (ρ ≈ .21–.37) and ''showed no measured effect on performance''; its effect on stress ''could not be tested''.

> ''Making monitoring visible measurably improved how people felt about the monitor. It did not measurably reduce the harm.''

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/the-exit-that-does-not-exist>>

!! No exit exists anywhere, and the closest thing is a petition

Across supervised-identity systems the subject's exit gates on the manager in every case: a child under 13 has ''none''; a managed enterprise account has ''none documented''; a supervised family member ''cannot leave the group''; a supervised teen must ''request'' and //"need your parent's approval to stop supervision"//.

''The closest thing that exists reads as a petition right that cannot be confiscated, never an exit'' — one platform lists, among what parents CANNOT do, //"Remove your ability to stop supervision"//. What stands non-removable is ''the ability to ASK''.

!!! ⚠ THE FIVE-YEAR HOLE

> Consent age 13–16; exit age 18, or 13–17 gated on a manager-controlled toggle. ''The consent age and the exit age are different numbers in every system reached, and the gap is manager-controlled.''

!!! The primitive that WOULD work has never been pointed at this relation

Social-recovery wallets carry the one design where the delegated party ''provably cannot become the controller'': the subject holds the signing key, guardians recover but never transact, and ''the subject removes guardians unilaterally after a timelock''. ⚠ It contains ''no supervised-principal role at all'', so there is no exit to block — the correct primitive exists and ''has never been applied to a manager→subject relation''.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/the-normative-floor>>

!! The only text requiring a channel the manager cannot see or block

A UN general comment on children's rights in the digital environment:

> ''¶75'' — digital surveillance of children //"should not be conducted routinely, indiscriminately or **without the child's knowledge**… nor should it take place **without the right to object to such surveillance**."//
> ''¶76'' — monitoring //"may prevent a child from accessing a helpline or searching for sensitive information."//
> ''¶77'' — protecting a child's privacy //"may be vital in circumstances where **parents or caregivers themselves pose a threat to the child's safety**"//.
> ''¶78'' — counselling providers //"should be **exempt from any requirement for a child user to obtain parental consent**."//

⚠ ''This is the only text anywhere requiring a channel the manager can neither see nor block. It has ZERO implementations.''

!!! And the law already treats monitoring as the conduct

A coercive-control statute names //"monitoring a person via online communication tools or using spyware"// among its conduct, and its prosecuting guidance carries the decisive sentence: //"**A victim may not know the full extent of a suspect's conduct, for instance if they were being monitored without their knowledge.**"// Its evidential list treats ''covert and overt tracking as equally evidential'' — never as different-severity conduct.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/two-instrument-lies>>

!! ⚠ Two instrument-lies the spirit caught, one of them live in a standards body

# ''A fetch-summarizer FABRICATED platform policy'' — presenting as quoted text a rule that monitoring apps must //"not operate covertly or without the knowledge of the person being tracked."// Disproved by raw-byte grep of the 238KB guidelines: `stalkerware` 0 · `monitor` 0 · `stalk` 0 · `covert` 0. ⚠ ''The summarizer invented a policy in exactly the direction a researcher would hope to find one.''
# ''A live IETF working-group draft misattributes three percentages'' (72% / 41% / 67%) to a paper that ''contains zero `%` characters'' — verified directly. Its real figures are n-counts. ''True source unidentified'', and the misattribution stands in an active document.

''Both cures ran the same way: fetch the raw bytes and count.''

<<~/ahu>>

